  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The National Authority for Alternative Dispute Resolution, abbreviated as &quot;NADR&quot;, was established by Royal Decree No. NS/RKT/1123/2381 dated November 2, 2023, as a complementary mechanism to the current dispute resolution system in Cambodia, aiming to improve thequality, efficiency, and speed of dispute resolution through out-of-court mediation and conciliation. The establishment of the NADRis part of the implementation of the justice system reform program in Cambodia, aiming to strengthen trust in thejustice system andpromote the right to justice for all individuals at the grassroots level and in the community in the Kingdom of Cambodia, through (1) maintaining the character of local dispute resolution in accordance with the actual needs of the citizens, and (2) ending disputes or settling disputes that occur at the grassroots level, aiming to contribute to reducing the number of cases in the courts. Over the past two years, the C.D.C. has accomplished a lot of work, both in terms of organizing institutional processes, including preparing a legal framework and selecting and training dispute resolution officers, and in terms of providing dispute resolution services to citizens. At the same time, with a clear vision to expand the scope of the C.D.C.&#x2019;swork to the people&#x2019;s living areas, at the 10thC.D.C. meeting on May 8, 2024, the C.D.C. reviewed the importance and specific needs of dispute resolution services, as well as thechallenges or obstacles faced by people living in indigenous and remote areas in accessing out-of-court dispute resolution services, including: (1)- Lack of skilled dispute resolution officers at sub-national administrations, while some officers have not receivedproper training.(2)- Dispute resolution by traditional authoritiesstill lacks both technical and legal understanding.(3)- The existing out-of-court dispute resolution mechanisms at the local level are still complicated, not smooth, and not responsive to the actual situation of citizens, especially indigenous peoples and people in remote areas, including:- There are still complications in filing and receiving complaints, especially in administrative procedures, which can make it difficult for citizens to receive good services.- Dispute resolution has many stages and is not coordinated, which can be a factor in the backlog of cases as the number of disputes increases.- Dispute resolution by local officials may lead to conflicts of interest involving dispute resolution mediators, which is also an obstacle to resolving disputes at the sub-national level in a transparent and impartial manner.- Dispute resolution mechanisms of national institutions mostly only receive complaints from citizens and forward the complaints or cases to local administrations (especially provincialadministrations) for review and resolution, which results in a large number of cases at the local level and can be congested.</narrative>
  </description>
